.\" openvpn-auth-imap.8 - manual page for the OpenVPN IMAP auth plugin
.\" License: MIT
.Dd November 13, 2026
.Dt OPENVPN-AUTH-IMAP 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm openvpn-auth-imap
.Nd authenticate OpenVPN users against IMAP servers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cm plugin
.Pa /usr/lib/openvpn/plugins/openvpn-auth-imap.so
.Cm server Ns = Ns Ar url
.Op Cm server Ns = Ns Ar url ...
.Op Ar option Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is an
.Xr openvpn 8
plugin that authenticates clients using
.Cm auth-user-pass
by logging in to one or more IMAP servers with the username and password
they supply.
A user is accepted only if an IMAP server confirms the password.
.Pp
The plugin is loaded with the
.Cm plugin
directive in the OpenVPN server configuration.
Its arguments are given on the same line as
.Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
pairs.
Clients need
.Cm auth-user-pass
in their configuration.
.Pp
OpenVPN passes the plugin the
.Ev username ,
.Ev password
and
.Ev auth_control_file .
The plugin double-forks, returns
.Dv OPENVPN_PLUGIN_FUNC_DEFERRED
immediately, and the background process tries the configured servers
through libcurl.
It then writes
.Ql 1
(accept) or
.Ql 0
(reject) to the control file, so the VPN server never blocks on IMAP.
Without an
.Ev auth_control_file ,
the check runs synchronously.
.Sh OPTIONS
Invalid arguments stop OpenVPN at startup with an error message,
rather than failing later at login time.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm server Ns = Ns Ar url
IMAP server to log in to.
Required; may be given up to 16 times.
Servers are tried in the order given.
.Ar url
is one of:
.Bl -tag -width "starttls://host[:port]"
.It Li imaps:// Ns Ar host Ns Op : Ns Ar port
Implicit TLS, default port 993.
.It Li starttls:// Ns Ar host Ns Op : Ns Ar port
STARTTLS, default port 143.
The login is refused if the server does not offer STARTTLS.
.It Li imap:// Ns Ar host Ns Op : Ns Ar port
Clear text, default port 143.
Passwords are sent unencrypted; a warning is logged at startup.
.El
.Pp
IPv6 addresses go in brackets, e.g.\&
.Li imaps://[2001:db8::1] .
.It Cm policy Ns = Ns Cm failover | any
How multiple servers are used.
.Bl -tag -width failover
.It Cm failover
(default) Try the next server only if the current one is unreachable or
returns an error.
A wrong password rejects the login at once.
Use this for redundant servers sharing one user database.
.It Cm any
Accept if any server accepts the credentials.
Use this when different servers hold different users.
.El
.It Cm cafile Ns = Ns Ar path
CA bundle used to verify server certificates.
Defaults to the system trust store.
.It Cm verify Ns = Ns Cm 0 | 1
Verify server certificates (default 1).
.Cm 0
disables verification and is meant for testing only.
.It Cm domain Ns = Ns Ar domain
Append
.Li @ Ns Ar domain
to usernames that contain no
.Ql @ .
.It Cm restrict-domain Ns = Ns Cm 0 | 1
With
.Cm 1 ,
reject logins that end up outside
.Cm domain Ns =
(e.g.\&
.Li bob@other.com )
before any server is contacted.
Requires
.Cm domain Ns = .
Without it, a username containing
.Ql @
is used as typed, so on an IMAP server hosting several domains every
mailbox of every domain can log in to the VPN.
Default 0.
.It Cm timeout Ns = Ns Ar seconds
Time allowed per server, 1 to 300 (default 10).
With
.Cm policy Ns = Ns Cm failover ,
the worst-case login time is roughly
.Ar timeout
times the number of servers; keep it well below OpenVPN's
.Cm hand-window
(60 seconds by default).
.It Cm max-pending Ns = Ns Ar n
Number of logins checked in the background at the same time,
1 to 4096 (default 64).
Further logins are rejected at once.
.It Cm match-cert Ns = Ns Cm 0 | 1
With
.Cm 1 ,
require the login to match the client certificate; see
.Sx CERTIFICATE BINDING .
Default 0.
.It Cm cert-map Ns = Ns Ar file
File listing certificates whose common name differs from the IMAP user;
see
.Sx FILES .
Requires
.Cm match-cert Ns = Ns Cm 1 .
.El
.Sh CERTIFICATE BINDING
With
.Cm match-cert Ns = Ns Cm 1 ,
a login is accepted only if the username matches the client certificate
the connection was made with.
A stolen password is then useless without the matching certificate.
Mismatches are rejected before any IMAP server is contacted.
.Pp
OpenVPN passes the certificate field selected by
.Cm x509-username-field
to the plugin as
.Ev common_name .
The plugin compares it with the login as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Direct match
The login must equal the common name, compared case-insensitively.
.Cm domain Ns =
applies to both, so a certificate
.Li alice
matches a login as
.Li alice
or
.Li alice@example.com .
.It Mapped certificates
A common name listed in the
.Cm cert-map
file may log in
.Em only
as its mapped users.
Names not in the map use the direct match.
.It Logging in with the common name
If the username typed is exactly the certificate's common name and that
name is in the map, the plugin logs in to IMAP as the mapped user.
If the name maps to several users the login is refused; the user must
type one of the mapped users instead.
.El
.Pp
The binding is only meaningful when OpenVPN verifies client certificates
against its CA, so never combine
.Cm match-cert
with
.Cm verify-client-cert none .
Do not use
.Cm username-as-common-name
either: it replaces the certificate name with the typed username,
which defeats the comparison.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /usr/lib/openvpn/plugins/openvpn-auth-imap.so
Default install location of the plugin.
It should be owned by root and not writable by other users.
.It Ar cert-map
Certificate map given with
.Cm cert-map Ns = .
Each line holds a common name followed by an IMAP user:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# common_name          imap-user
vpn-laptop-07          alice@example.com
# common names may contain spaces
John Smith             carol
# repeat a name to allow several users
shared-kiosk           alice@example.com
shared-kiosk           bob@example.com
.Ed
.Pp
The user is the last whitespace-separated field; everything before it is
the common name.
Common names are compared exactly (case-sensitive), users
case-insensitively, and
.Cm domain Ns =
applies to mapped users without
.Ql @ .
Blank lines and lines starting with
.Ql #
are ignored; comments cannot follow an entry on the same line.
.Pp
The file is read once at startup; restart OpenVPN or send it
.Dv SIGHUP
after editing it.
A missing or malformed file stops OpenVPN with the file name and line
number.
The file must be owned by root or the user OpenVPN runs as and must not
be writable by others, otherwise OpenVPN refuses to start; a
group-writable file triggers a warning.
If OpenVPN drops privileges with
.Cm user Ns / Ns Cm group ,
the file must be readable by that user for a
.Dv SIGHUP
reload to work.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Two redundant servers, bare usernames completed with a domain:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
plugin /usr/lib/openvpn/plugins/openvpn-auth-imap.so \e
    server=imaps://mail1.example.com \e
    server=imaps://mail2.example.com domain=example.com
.Ed
.Pp
Username and password only, without client certificates:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
verify-client-cert none
username-as-common-name
.Ed
.Pp
Logins bound to client certificates, with a certificate map:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
plugin /usr/lib/openvpn/plugins/openvpn-auth-imap.so \e
    server=imaps://mail.example.com domain=example.com \e
    match-cert=1 cert-map=/etc/openvpn/cert-map
x509-username-field CN
.Ed
.Pp
The backslash continuations above are for readability only; OpenVPN
expects the
.Cm plugin
directive on a single line.
.Pp
Checking credentials against an IMAP server by hand; if this succeeds,
the plugin accepts the same credentials:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
curl -v --user 'alice@example.com:secret' -X NOOP imaps://mail.example.com/
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr curl 1 ,
.Xr openvpn 8
.Sh BUGS
Some STARTTLS servers advertise
.Li LOGINDISABLED
before TLS and offer only the
.Li LOGIN
command afterwards, with no SASL mechanisms.
With these, some libcurl versions (e.g. 8.5.0) refuse to log in, so
valid users are rejected.
Use
.Li imaps://
instead.
.Pp
Report bugs at
.Lk https://github.com/snbsrl/openvpn-auth-imap/issues
